+### Best practice: name every interval and every repeated value
+
+When a protocol is described in terms of **trials of specific lengths** ("a 4-second
+stimulus, then a 2-second blank, repeated 20 times"), **do not** hard-code those numbers
+into the command fields. Lift each one into a descriptively named variable and bind the
+field(s) to it. This is the single highest-leverage habit for a readable, editable,
+drift-free protocol.
+
+Rules of thumb, in priority order:
+
+1. **Every timing value is a variable.** Trial durations, blank/ITI durations, hold
+   windows, closed-loop durations, pre/post pulse gaps — all of it. Timing is what
+   experimenters tune most, and (per THE WAITS RULE) a duration almost always drives two
+   fields (`trialParams.duration` **and** its companion `wait.duration`). Binding both to
+   one anchor is the only way they can't silently drift.
+2. **Anything that recurs more than once becomes a variable.** If a literal appears in two
+   or more places — a speed/`frame_rate`, a `gain`, an LED `power`, a `pattern_ID`, an
+   analog `mv` level, a repetition count — anchor it. The moment a value is written twice,
+   a future edit will update one copy and miss the other; an anchor makes that impossible.
+3. **Name for meaning, not for the number.** `&stim_dur 4` / `&blank_dur 2` /
+   `&grating_speed_hz 5`, never `&four` or `&d1`. A good name says what the interval *is*
+   and (where useful) its unit — matching the repo's existing style (`trial_dur`,
+   `open_dur`, `cl_dur`, `inter_wait`, `fr_rate`). When the described protocol names the
+   phases ("stimulus", "inter-trial", "adaptation"), reuse those words in the anchor names.
+4. **One concept, one anchor.** If two fields are *conceptually* the same interval, share
+   an anchor (the waits-rule pair). If two fields merely happen to hold the same number
+   today but are independent knobs (e.g. a stimulus duration that coincidentally equals a
+   blank duration), give them **separate** anchors — otherwise tuning one drags the other.
+
+Worked translation — the description *"show a grating for 4 s at 5 Hz, then a 2 s blank,
+20 trials"* becomes:
+
+```yaml
+variables:
+  - &stim_dur 4          # grating on-time (s)
+  - &blank_dur 2         # inter-trial blank (s)
+  - &grating_speed_hz 5  # frame rate, mode 2 (Hz)
+  - &n_trials 20         # block repetitions
+conditions:
+  - name: "grating_stim"
+    commands:
+      - type: "controller"
+        command_name: "trialParams"
+        pattern: "G6_2x10_grating_20px"
+        pattern_ID: 2
+        duration: *stim_dur          # ← both bound to the same interval
+        mode: 2
+        frame_index: 0
+        frame_rate: *grating_speed_hz
+        gain: 0
+      - type: "wait"
+        duration: *stim_dur          # ← waits rule: same anchor, can't drift
+  - name: "blank"
+    commands:
+      - type: "wait"
+        duration: *blank_dur
+experiment:
+  - name: "main_block"
+    trials: ["grating_stim"]
+    repetitions: *n_trials
+    intertrial: "blank"
+```
+
+Every number an experimenter would want to change now lives in one place at the top of the
+file, self-documenting via its name. A reviewer can read the `variables:` block alone and
+know the whole timing structure of the experiment.
