 ## Plugins
 
-`plugins:` normally comes from the rig (the Studio pre-fills New protocols from the rig
-YAML; CSHL course rigs declare none). The **web runner executes only `fictrac` and `log`**
-— `backlight`/`camera`/`temperature` are declared for MATLAB and skipped on the web.
-FicTrac closed loop = a `fictrac` plugin + a Mode-3 condition.
+Plugins are named devices/services (a camera, a backlight, the FicTrac bridge) that a
+condition can command alongside the arena. **Source of truth for every plugin and its
+commands is `js/plugin-registry.js`** — the tables below mirror it; if they ever disagree,
+the registry wins.
+
+### How a plugin command looks in YAML
+
+A command in a condition's `commands:` array is one of three `type`s — `controller`,
+`wait`, or **`plugin`**. A plugin command names the plugin and its command and passes that
+command's params inline under `params:`:
+
+```yaml
+      - type: "plugin"
+        plugin_name: "camera"          # must match a declared plugin's name
+        command_name: "startRecording"
+        params:
+          filename: "fly3_trial1"      # only the params that command defines
+```
+
+Plugin commands (and `log`) are **instantaneous** — like the I/O controller commands they
+do NOT advance the clock, so a plugin action that should overlap a trial still needs the
+trial's own `wait`. Command lookup is by the plugin's `matlab.class`, so importing a
+condition is name-agnostic; but **config inheritance from the rig is by NAME**, so keep the
+canonical names below (`backlight`, `camera`, `temperature`, `fictrac`).
+
+### Where the plugin list comes from — the rig YAML
+
+`plugins:` in the protocol is normally pre-filled by the Studio from the **rig YAML** the
+user selects (Settings → Rig). The rig file is the declaration of *what hardware this bench
+physically has*; each plugin it lists with `enabled: true` is offered in the designer, and
+carries the connection config (ports, IPs, bridge URL) so the protocol doesn't have to:
+
+```yaml
+# in the rig YAML (e.g. configs/rigs/cshl_g6_2x10_ball.yaml)
+plugins:
+  fictrac:                 # ← the KEY is the canonical plugin name
+    enabled: true          # ← present-on-this-rig signal; the designer offers it
+    type: "fictrac"
+    bridge_url: "ws://localhost:8765"
+    fictrac_port: 60000
+```
+
+Consequences of this design:
+- A plugin declared on the rig is a **capability signal**: the designer offers it, and it
+  **flags an experiment as unsupported on a rig that lacks it** (e.g. a FicTrac protocol on
+  a rig with no `fictrac`). The diff is name-based (`diffRigVsProtocol`).
+- The protocol plugin's `name` must equal the rig key for the rig's config to carry over —
+  this is why the thermometer is keyed **`temperature`** (not `thermometer`) everywhere.
+- Rig-provided config fields (a camera `ip`/`port`, a serial `port`) are marked `rigDefined`
+  and are **omitted from the exported protocol** — they live in the rig file, not the YAML.
+- **Course/teaching rigs may declare no plugins at all** (the CSHL 2×10 non-ball rig). AO
+  LED drive / digital-out are *controller-native* commands, NOT plugins — they need no
+  `plugins:` entry, only G6 hardware.
+- The four canonical rig-plugin names (`backlight`, `camera`, `temperature`, `fictrac`) are
+  a built-in baseline, recognized even when no rig is loaded.
+
+### What actually runs on the web vs. MATLAB
+
+The **web runner executes only `fictrac` and `log`.** `backlight` / `camera` /
+`temperature` commands are declared for the MATLAB executor and are **parsed and skipped**
+on the web (they're valid to include — they just no-op in the browser). Author them when
+the protocol will also run under MATLAB; don't expect them to fire from the web Studio.
+
+### Command catalog (from `js/plugin-registry.js`)
+
+**`backlight`** — `LEDControllerPlugin` (MATLAB-side; visible/IR LED backlights):
+
+| command | params |
+|---|---|
+| `setIRLEDPower` | `power` 0–100 |
+| `setRedLEDPower` / `setGreenLEDPower` / `setBlueLEDPower` | `power` 0–100; `panel_num` (0=all); `pattern` (e.g. `"1010"`, optional) |
+| `turnOnLED` / `turnOffLED` | — |
+| `setVisibleBacklightsOff` | — (kills RGB, keeps IR on) |
+
+**`camera`** — `BiasPlugin` (MATLAB-side; BIAS video):
+
+| command | params |
+|---|---|
+| `connect` | `ip`, `port`, `config_file` (all optional; use when rig omits them) |
+| `startRecording` | `filename` (optional) |
+| `stopRecording` / `startPreview` / `stopCapture` / `getTimestamp` / `disconnect` | — |
+
+**`temperature`** — `DAQThermometerPlugin` (MATLAB-side; NI-DAQ thermocouples):
+
+| command | params |
+|---|---|
+| `startContinuousLogging` / `stopContinuousLogging` | — (background CSV logging; best for long runs) |
+| `get_temperature` | — (one blocking sample) |
+| `log_temperature` | — (read + log one sample, optional PNG) |
+
+**`fictrac`** — closed-loop fly-on-ball (**web-executed** via the local bridge):
+
+| command | params |
+|---|---|
+| `connect` / `disconnect` | — (idempotent; runner auto-connects at run start) |
+| `startClosedLoop` | `gain` (optional override, deg/index) — requires a preceding Mode-3 `trialParams` |
+| `stopClosedLoop` | — (FicTrac keeps logging) |
+
+**`log`** — built-in, **always available, never declared** in `plugins:` (**web-executed**):
+
+| command | params |
+|---|---|
+| `log` | `message` (required, ≤2000 chars); `level` = DEBUG/INFO/WARNING/ERROR (default INFO) |
+
+FicTrac closed loop = a `fictrac` plugin + a Mode-3 condition (see the Mode-3 section above).
 
